# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
fever | definition | A fever is a body temperature above about 38 degrees Celsius, and it is a symptom rather than an illness in itself, because the immune system raises temperature to make conditions worse for invading germs. Rest and fluids help, and paracetamol or ibuprofen can help if you can take them safely. The pattern matters more than the number on the thermometer, namely how long it lasted and what else came with it. See a doctor if a fever lasts more than three days, or comes with chest pain, a stiff neck, confusion, or a rash that does not fade under pressure. | fever, temperature, thermometer, chills, kataas | what is a fever, how high is too high a fever, when should i see a doctor for a fever | lagnat | lagnat
dehydration | definition | Dehydration happens when the body loses more fluid than it takes in, and it is more dangerous in children and older people than in most adults. Thirst is already a late signal, so a practical guide is to watch the colour of urine instead, where pale straw means well hydrated and dark amber means you have been going a while without. Dizziness on standing, a dry mouth, low urine output or confusion are reasons to seek help rather than simply drink more water. | dehydration, water, hydration, urine, thirsty, tubig | how do i know if i am dehydrated, what colour should urine be, how much water should i drink | tubig | tubig
headache | definition | Most headaches are tension-type or migraine, and both are diagnosed from a pattern of symptoms rather than a scan. A tension headache is a dull, tight band-like pain, usually worse later in the day, often with neck and shoulder tension. A migraine is typically one-sided, throbbing, worsened by movement, and often comes with nausea and sensitivity to light or sound. The red flags that need a doctor rather than a painkiller are a sudden severe headache unlike any previous one, a headache with fever and a stiff neck, weakness or vision loss, or one that follows a head injury. | headache, migraine, tension, head, ulo, mata | what causes headaches, how do i know if it is a migraine, when is a headache dangerous | ulo | ulo
back pain | definition | Most back pain is mechanical, coming from muscles and joints asked to do more than they can, and it settles within weeks without treatment. The changes that help are unglamorous, such as keeping moving rather than resting flat on a bed, building up the muscles around the spine, and setting screens and desks so you are not folded over a desk all day. Sitting is not by itself dangerous and no single posture is the villain, the awkward position you can hold for a long time is the problem. Sudden severe pain after a fall, numbness around the saddle, or loss of bladder or bowel control are emergencies. | back, pain, posture, spine, likod | how do i fix my posture, why does my back hurt, is sitting bad for your back | likod | likud
allergy | definition | An allergy is an immune reaction to a substance that is harmless to most people, such as pollen, dust mites, animal dander or certain foods. The immune system treats the trigger as a threat and produces antibodies that cause sneezing, itching, hives or swelling. Severity ranges from a seasonal nuisance to anaphylaxis, which is a medical emergency requiring immediate adrenaline and hospital care. Food allergies are one of the conditions that genuinely requires strict avoidance, and the number of children affected has risen sharply over the past few decades for reasons still debated. | allergy, allergic, pollen, anaphylaxis, histamine | what is an allergy, can allergies go away, what is anaphylaxis | alerji | alerji
insomnia | definition | Insomnia is difficulty falling asleep or staying asleep when there is the opportunity to do so, and the definition includes the opportunity because being unable to sleep in a train is not insomnia. It is a complaint rather than a disease, and the modern approach treats it that way: the most common cause is worry and a brain that is still alert rather than tired, and everything done to fight it, such as lying there for hours or watching a screen, teaches the bed that it is a place for being awake. What helps is a consistent wake-up time, daylight in the morning, a fixed wind-down, and not treating the clock. Medication helps in the short term and is generally not the first answer. In the Philippines, a shared and thin-walled house, a hot room and a neighbour's shift work are ordinary causes rather than psychological ones, and a person who lives in that situation should not be told the problem is their attitude. The measure that matters is daytime function, since sleeping five hours and coping well is a different situation from sleeping seven and being tired. | sleep, awake, bed, wake, habit, tired, function | what is insomnia, how do you fix insomnia, is insomnia serious | insomnia | insomnia

